          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Apply Water-Based Polyurethane to Floors: The Complete DIY Guide
          </h2>
          <p>
            Applying water-based poly to a floor is well within reach for a careful DIYer, but the fast dry time makes a few steps non-negotiable. Here's the process that gives professional results:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Sand the Floor</h3>
          <p>
            Sand the floor smooth, working up through the grits to a final pass with 100-120 grit. The goal is a clean, even, scratch-free surface — the finish only looks as good as the wood underneath it. On a previously finished floor, sand until the old finish is gone and you're down to bare, uniform wood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Vacuum and Tack</h3>
          <p>
            This is the step that separates a glassy finish from a gritty one. Vacuum the entire floor thoroughly, then go over it with a slightly damp microfiber cloth or a water-dampened tack cloth to lift every last speck of dust. Any grit left behind gets locked under the finish forever. Don't skip the corners and baseboards.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Apply Thin, Even Coats</h3>
          <p>
            Cut in the edges with a synthetic brush, then pull the finish across the floor with a T-bar or microfiber applicator, working in the direction of the grain and keeping a wet edge so you don't get lap marks. Apply <strong>thin, even coats</strong> — flooding it on causes puddles, bubbles, and a cloudy cure. Water-based poly raises the grain on the first coat, so expect the floor to feel slightly rough afterward; that's normal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Sand Between Coats with 220</h3>
          <p>
            Once each coat is dry (typically 2-4 hours), scuff-sand lightly with <strong>220-grit</strong> sandpaper or a fine sanding screen to knock down raised grain and give the next coat something to grip. Vacuum and tack again after sanding. This light abrasion between every coat is the secret to a smooth, durable, professional-looking floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Build Three Coats (Four for High Traffic)</h3>
          <p>
            Apply a minimum of <strong>three coats</strong> of water-based polyurethane, scuff-sanding between each. On busy floors — hallways, kitchens, entryways — add a fourth coat to make up for water-based poly being slightly less durable per coat than oil. Thanks to the fast recoat, you can usually finish all three coats in a single day. Then let it cure: light foot traffic after 24 hours, but wait 7-14 days before replacing rugs and heavy furniture.
          </p>
        </div>

