          </p>
          <p>
            Maintenance is simple and keeps the finish looking new for a decade. Dust-mop or vacuum regularly to remove the grit that acts like sandpaper underfoot. Wipe spills promptly, and clean with a pH-neutral hardwood floor cleaner — never vinegar, ammonia, or steam mops, all of which dull and damage the finish over time. Use felt pads under furniture legs, place mats at entrances to catch grit, and keep pet nails trimmed. When the finish eventually starts to dull in traffic lanes after several years, you can usually <strong>screen and recoat</strong> — a light scuff-sand and one fresh coat — rather than sanding back to bare wood, which is one more advantage of a quality water-based system.
          </p>
